[al:新概念英语（四）]
[ar:MP3 同步字幕版（美音）]
[ti:How to Grow Old]
[by:更多学习内容，请到VeryCD.com搜索“露珠”]
[00:00.40]Lesson 11 | 第11课
[00:02.44]How to grow old | 如何安度晚年
[00:10.60]What, according to the author, is the best way to overcome the fear of death as you get older? | 按照作者的观点，随着年龄增长，克服怕死心理的最好办法是什么？
[00:20.22]Some old people are oppressed by the fear of death. | 有些老年人因怕死而感到压抑。
[00:25.14]In the young there is a justification for this feeling. | 在年轻人身上有这种感觉情有可原。
[00:28.83]Young men who have reason to fear that they will be killed in battle | 有理由害怕自己会死在战场上的年轻人，
[00:33.24]may justifiably feel bitter in the thought | 想到自己被剥夺了生活所能给予的最美好的东西而感到痛苦，
[00:36.79]that they have been cheated of the best things that life has to offer. | 这是无可指责的。
[00:42.45]But in an old man who has known human joys and sorrows, | 但老年人已经尝尽了人生的欢乐与忧愁，
[00:48.12]and has achieved whatever work it was in him to do, | 做完了他力所能及的一切工作，
[00:52.01]the fear of death is somewhat abject and ignoble. | 那么怕死就有点儿卑鄙和不光彩了。
[00:56.93]The best way to overcome it--so at least it seems to me-- | 克服怕死心理的最好办法——至少在我看来是这样——
[01:02.67]is to make your interests gradually wider and more impersonal, | 就是使自己的兴趣逐渐变得更广泛，更超脱个人，
[01:08.05]until bit by bit the walls of the ego recede, | 直到自我的围墙一点点地消失，
[01:13.22]and your life becomes increasingly merged in the universal life. | 而自己的生活越来越融合于全人类的生活之中。
[01:17.73]An individual human existence should be like a river-- | 个人的存在应当像一条河流——
[01:23.51]small at first, narrowly contained within its banks, | 开始时细小，局限在两岸之间，
[01:28.42]and rushing passionately past boulders and over waterfalls. | 然后热情地冲过巨石，飞下瀑布。
[01:33.70]Gradually the river grows wider, the banks recede, the waters flow more quietly, | 河流渐渐地变宽，两岸后撤，水面流得更平静，
[01:41.18]and in the end, without any visiblebreak, they become merged in the sea, | 最后，看不出有任何断裂，河水汇入了大海，
[01:47.44]and painlessly lose their individual being. | 无痛无痒地失去了个体的存在。
[01:51.19]The man who, in old age can see his life in this way, | 在老年时能这样看待生活的人，
[01:56.77]will not suffer from the fear of death, | 不会遭受怕死的痛苦，
[01:59.54]since the things he cares for will continue. | 因为他所关怀的事物将继续下去。
[02:03.38]And if, with the decay of vitality, weariness increases, | 而如果随着精力的衰退，倦怠之感日增，
[02:10.52]the thought of rest will be not unwelcome. | 长眠的想法未必不受欢迎。
[02:14.29]I should wish to die while still at work, | 我希望在工作时死去，
[02:17.82]knowing that others will carry on what I can no longer do, | 知道别人将接替我不能再做的工作，
[02:22.36]and content in the thought that what was possible has been done. | 并且想到已经做成了可能做到的事情而感到满足。
